export const metadata = { title: "Security model \u2014 Glaux docs" };

export default function Page() {
  return <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: "<h1 id=\"security-model\">Security model</h1>\n<p>Glaux executes model-written code, so isolation is a core feature rather than an\nafterthought. This page describes what the system defends and what it deliberately does\nnot. To <em>report</em> a vulnerability, see\n<a href=\"https://github.com/aniketshukla1/glaux/blob/master/SECURITY.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">SECURITY.md</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layers\">Layers</h2>\n<h3 id=\"1-static-capability-check\">1. Static capability check</h3>\n<p>Before a synthesized skill ever runs, its source is checked against the capabilities it\ndeclared. Code reaching for authority it wasn't granted (network, filesystem, process\nspawning) is rejected \u2014 it never reaches execution.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-sandbox\">2. The sandbox</h3>\n<p>Skills run in a <strong>WebAssembly guest with no ambient authority</strong>: it cannot syscall by\ndefault, so there is nothing to escape <em>to</em> unless a capability was explicitly granted.\nRunaway code is bounded by both fuel (instruction budget) and wall-clock limits. A\nhardened subprocess sandbox is the fallback backend where WASM isn't available.</p>\n<p>This has been exercised adversarially \u2014 attempts to open sockets, read <code>/etc/passwd</code>, or\nspin in an infinite loop are contained.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-workspace-jail\">3. The workspace jail</h3>\n<p>File access is a <em>separate</em>, opt-in capability. When enabled, reads and writes are\nconfined to one directory, with per-file and total size caps, and guards against path\ntraversal, absolute paths, and symlinks pointing outside. The workspace root itself\ncannot be deleted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-identity-and-capability-tokens\">4. Identity and capability tokens</h3>\n<p>Each agent holds an Ed25519 keypair. Cross-agent delegation carries a capability token\nthat the receiving agent verifies \u2014 it refuses work that wasn't issued to it, is expired,\nor is for a capability it wasn't granted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-the-ledger\">5. The ledger</h3>\n<p>Every action is signed by the acting agent and hash-chained. Undetected tampering means\nbreaking the chain. This is what makes \"prove it\" more than a slogan: you can demonstrate\nwhat the system did, including how it modified itself.</p>\n<p><code>glaux verify --file &lt;path&gt;</code> re-checks a chain straight off disk \u2014 no running server,\nand no trust in the app that wrote it. Try to forge it and see:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sed -i.bak '2s/&quot;agent_id&quot;:&quot;[^&quot;]*&quot;/&quot;agent_id&quot;:&quot;mallory&quot;/' ~/.glaux/ledger.jsonl\nglaux verify --file ~/.glaux/ledger.jsonl\n#  \u2717 CHAIN INVALID at block 1 \u2014 block hash mismatch\n</code></pre>\n<p>It exits <code>0</code> on a valid chain and <code>1</code> on a broken one, so it drops straight into CI or an\naudit script. Re-signing the forged block doesn't help: signatures are checked against the\nacting agent's key, and every later block hashes the one before it.</p>\n<p><strong>What this proves, precisely:</strong> integrity and attribution \u2014 this record was produced by\nthese agents, in this order, and has not been altered since. It does <em>not</em> prove the\ncontent is correct. A wrong answer stays wrong; it just becomes non-repudiable and\nimpossible to quietly revise after the fact.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-human-in-the-loop-controls\">6. Human-in-the-loop controls</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>glaux approval gated</code> \u2014 a human approves each synthesized skill before it can run.</li>\n<li><code>glaux safety dry-run</code> \u2014 execute reasoning, skip side effects.</li>\n<li><code>glaux safety halted</code> \u2014 stop all execution.</li>\n<li>Self-modification is gated, signed, and reversible by design.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"deliberate-trade-offs\">Deliberate trade-offs</h2>\n<p>These are design decisions, not gaps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The workspace grants real file access</strong> inside its jail when you enable it. That is\n  its purpose; the boundary is the jail, not the absence of access.</li>\n<li><strong>MCP servers you connect run with the authority you give them.</strong> Glaux cannot sandbox\n  a third-party tool server \u2014 treat connecting one like installing a plugin.</li>\n<li><strong>The semantic answer cache cannot see conversation context</strong>, so it is off by default\n  and its similarity threshold is deliberately strict. Time-sensitive directives are\n  never cached.</li>\n<li><strong><code>dry-run</code> and <code>halted</code> are cooperative controls</strong> over the swarm's own execution\n  path. They are not a containment mechanism against code that has already escaped the\n  sandbox \u2014 that is the sandbox's job.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"handling-secrets\">Handling secrets</h2>\n<p>API keys live in your environment or under <code>GLAUX_HOME</code> (git-ignored) and are never\nwritten to the ledger or logs. Skill arguments are truncated before being signed, so a\nlarge payload can't bloat \u2014 or leak through \u2014 a block. A secrets broker mediates skill\naccess to credentials rather than handing them to guest code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"operating-glaux-safely\">Operating Glaux safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Start with capabilities off; enable only what a workload needs.</li>\n<li>Use <code>approval gated</code> when running untrusted or high-stakes directives.</li>\n<li>Keep <code>GLAUX_API_SECRET</code> set if the server is reachable beyond localhost.</li>\n<li>Run <code>glaux verify</code> as part of any audit.</li>\n</ul>" }} />;
}
